Bug surfaced from user testing: typing a model in the Profile→Change modal
silently wrote model: null back to xpressclaw.yaml, and a second Apply
without retyping the API key wiped api_key too. Reproduced live by tailing
~/.xpressclaw/xpressclaw.yaml with `tail -F` (capital F — xpressclaw rewrites
the file, so lowercase -f misses it).

Root cause (took two tries to land on):

The per-agent LLM refactor 0b83c91 moved `model` from top-level AgentConfig
into AgentLlmConfig.model — "model lives on llm.model now." The PATCH
handler in routes/agents.rs grew a legacy migration bridge to keep
pre-refactor API callers working (top-level `model` -> write into
llm.model). That branch coexisted with an `llm` block branch that did a
full agent.llm = req.llm replacement. Two branches, both correct in
isolation, but the modal in ProfileTab.svelte was a NEW caller that used
BOTH at once: it sent top-level `model` AND an `llm` block (without model
inside it). The legacy branch wrote model; the next branch's full
replacement clobbered it. The original code's comment literally said "so
the field never gets silently dropped" — the author saw the risk and
guarded against it but didn't notice the second block undoing the guard.

My first fix attempt was a server-side partial merge with filter-on-empty
semantics (req_llm.api_key.filter(|k| !k.is_empty()) etc). User correctly
called it a hack — the server was contorting itself to compensate for the
frontend sending a malformed payload. The cleaner answer is to align the
modal with the data model: model lives inside the llm block, so the modal
should send it that way. Then the dual-branch server handler becomes
unnecessary, not "fixed."

Final shape:
- Modal applyModelConfig sends model INSIDE the llm block.
- handleSave (Save Changes) drops top-level `model` entirely. Model is
  the modal's responsibility. Both blocks had been writing it, which is
  also why a Save Changes click after a modal edit could race.
- Server: dropped `model: Option<String>` from UpdateAgentConfigRequest
  AND the migration block. Handler is one block now — req.llm replaces
  agent.llm wholesale, empty/null provider clears.
- YAML migration for old configs with top-level agent.model is
  unaffected — Config::migrate_legacy_fields() still handles it at file
  load. Migration belongs at the file boundary, not in an API handler.
- PATCH response returns "api_key": <value> instead of "has_api_key":
  bool — matches the GET /api/setup/config shape. Frontend was using
  has_api_key inconsistently anyway (only the AgentProviderEntry
  summary type cared about it).

Rejected alternatives:
- Partial-merge with filter-on-empty (hack, server compensating for
  frontend).
- Keep dual-branch but reorder (still hacky, comment lies about modal
  shape after frontend fix).
- Use serde_with::double_option to distinguish None vs Some(None) on
  request fields — overkill for one field; would have introduced a new
  pattern that doesn't exist anywhere else in the codebase.
- Force the api_key field to be opaque in the response (write-only) —
  would have required reworking AgentLlmConfig and breaking GET symmetry.

Tests: new test_update_config_llm_block_round_trips_model_and_key covers
the modal's actual contract (model + api_key persist on a single round-
trip; response carries api_key back). Dropped the migration-bridge test
I'd added earlier — that path no longer exists in the API. 10/10 agent
route tests pass.

Verified by tailing the YAML during the actual UI flow against a freshly
built installer (target/x86_64-pc-windows-msvc/release/bundle/nsis/
xpressclaw_0.1.0_x64-setup.exe). The agent responded post-fix but the
first message after a fresh install took ~3 min — cold-start tax from
